// run
// `stackMapsBegin()`/`stackMapsEnd()` (§11.12): the half-open extent of the
// compiler-emitted stack-map table (ABI.md §4).
//
// Neither name is defined by any object — the table is the concatenation of
// every contributing member's `.bit_gc`/`__DATA,__bit_gc` atoms, so only the
// LINKER knows the total. This case is therefore an end-to-end proof that the
// undefined external actually resolves, not just that it type-checks: the
// addresses are read at run time out of a fully linked image.
//
// The `while` loop is load-bearing, not filler. Both backends synthesize the
// `bit_rt_safepoint` call ONLY at a loop back edge, so a straight-line `main`
// would contain no safepoint at all and the table would legitimately be able to
// hold no entry for it. The loop is what makes this "a linked program that
// contains at least one safepoint".
//
// Only relations are printed, never an address: Bit's ELF output is a static
// non-relocated image (absolute addresses reproduce), but the Mach-O output is
// position independent and the loader slides it, so a literal address would
// differ between runs. The relations below hold on both.
// Both reads sit inside a `@nosplit` body on purpose (§10.3.1): the only caller
// that can ever exist is the collector's root walk, which runs from a safepoint
// poll and is therefore nosplit by construction. Admitting them there rests on
// the same PROOF `entryOf` is admitted on — one inline address materialization
// against a link-time constant, so neither can allocate or reach a safepoint.
@nosplit fn mapsBegin(): int {
  return int(stackMapsBegin())
}

@nosplit fn mapsEnd(): int {
  return int(stackMapsEnd())
}

fn main() {
  const begin = mapsBegin()
  const end = mapsEnd()

  let sum = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < 4) {
    sum = sum + i
    i = i + 1
  }

  print("loop=${sum}\n")
  print("begin_nonzero=${begin != 0}\n")
  print("end_nonzero=${end != 0}\n")
  // Half-open and non-empty: this program has a safepoint, so the table it
  // bounds cannot be the empty run.
  print("nonempty=${end > begin}\n")
  // The two calls name link-time constants, so each is invariant within a run.
  print("stable=${mapsBegin() == begin && mapsEnd() == end}\n")
}
